1
00:00:01,150 --> 00:00:03,830
Case number zero zero seven.

2
00:00:03,950 --> 00:00:04,590
Subject:

3
00:00:04,190 --> 00:00:05,550
the honeybee.

4
00:00:05,870 --> 00:00:06,490
Charge:

5
00:00:06,090 --> 00:00:07,650
keeping the world fed.

6
00:00:08,290 --> 00:00:09,790
This is the file.

7
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:11,630
Exhibit one.

8
00:00:11,690 --> 00:00:12,690
The numbers.

9
00:00:13,170 --> 00:00:15,750
There are more than twenty thousand species of

10
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:15,990
bees.

11
00:00:16,250 --> 00:00:17,730
Most of them are wild,

12
00:00:17,630 --> 00:00:19,590
and most of them you have never met.

13
00:00:19,950 --> 00:00:22,570
And according to United Nations food experts,

14
00:00:22,470 --> 00:00:24,710
about a third of the world's food production

15
00:00:24,310 --> 00:00:25,730
depends on bees.

16
00:00:26,270 --> 00:00:28,190
One in every three bites.

17
00:00:28,370 --> 00:00:30,590
Think about that the next time you eat.

18
00:00:30,870 --> 00:00:32,390
Exhibit two.

19
00:00:32,530 --> 00:00:33,510
The dance.

20
00:00:33,890 --> 00:00:35,850
A worker finds flowers.

21
00:00:36,130 --> 00:00:37,370
She flies home,

22
00:00:36,970 --> 00:00:38,210
and she dances.

23
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:40,450
Her body points the way,

24
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:41,750
measured against the sun.

25
00:00:41,770 --> 00:00:43,330
The longer she waggles,

26
00:00:43,110 --> 00:00:44,770
the farther the flowers are.

27
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:46,750
The other bees read it,

28
00:00:46,350 --> 00:00:48,470
and they fly straight to the food.

29
00:00:48,970 --> 00:00:52,190
A scientist named Karl von Frisch decoded that

30
00:00:51,790 --> 00:00:52,610
dance.

31
00:00:52,590 --> 00:00:54,010
In 1973,

32
00:00:53,830 --> 00:00:56,450
it helped earn him a Nobel Prize.

33
00:00:57,070 --> 00:00:59,670
The bees had been speaking the whole time.

34
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:01,870
We just had to learn the language.

35
00:01:02,730 --> 00:01:03,750
Exhibit three.

36
00:01:03,930 --> 00:01:04,730
The vote.

37
00:01:05,250 --> 00:01:07,210
When a colony outgrows its home,

38
00:01:07,250 --> 00:01:09,550
thousands of bees leave with the old queen

39
00:01:09,150 --> 00:01:10,970
and hang together in a cluster.

40
00:01:11,510 --> 00:01:12,990
They need a new home,

41
00:01:12,770 --> 00:01:13,490
fast.

42
00:01:13,570 --> 00:01:16,230
So scouts go out and inspect the options.

43
00:01:16,730 --> 00:01:19,150
Each scout comes back and dances for the

44
00:01:18,750 --> 00:01:19,910
place she found.

45
00:01:19,990 --> 00:01:22,350
A great site gets a long dance.

46
00:01:22,430 --> 00:01:24,830
A poor site gets a short one.

47
00:01:24,950 --> 00:01:26,510
She does not oversell.

48
00:01:27,090 --> 00:01:29,270
Other scouts go and check her work.

49
00:01:29,710 --> 00:01:32,070
And when about fifteen scouts are standing at

50
00:01:31,670 --> 00:01:33,650
the same site at the same time,

51
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:35,010
the decision is made.

52
00:01:35,090 --> 00:01:37,570
The whole swarm lifts off and moves.

53
00:01:38,150 --> 00:01:39,210
No king.

54
00:01:39,390 --> 00:01:41,070
No campaign money.

55
00:01:41,210 --> 00:01:42,770
Just honest reports,

56
00:01:42,370 --> 00:01:43,630
and a quorum.

57
00:01:44,090 --> 00:01:45,530
Exhibit four.

58
00:01:45,610 --> 00:01:46,750
The heat ball.

59
00:01:47,290 --> 00:01:48,150
In Japan,

60
00:01:48,090 --> 00:01:50,390
a giant hornet can wipe out a hive.

61
00:01:50,430 --> 00:01:52,990
So the Japanese honeybee has a defense.

62
00:01:53,630 --> 00:01:56,210
Five hundred bees or more surround the hornet

63
00:01:55,810 --> 00:01:57,090
in a tight ball,

64
00:01:56,690 --> 00:01:59,130
and they vibrate their flight muscles.

65
00:01:59,690 --> 00:02:02,310
The ball heats to about forty seven degrees

66
00:02:01,910 --> 00:02:02,930
Celsius.

67
00:02:03,110 --> 00:02:05,030
Hot enough to kill the hornet.

68
00:02:05,010 --> 00:02:07,050
Not hot enough to kill the bees.

69
00:02:07,670 --> 00:02:10,310
No single bee can beat that hornet.

70
00:02:10,130 --> 00:02:12,390
Five hundred together cook it.

71
00:02:13,030 --> 00:02:14,130
Exhibit five.

72
00:02:14,310 --> 00:02:15,310
The colony.

73
00:02:15,930 --> 00:02:16,690
In summer,

74
00:02:16,290 --> 00:02:19,030
a colony can hold sixty thousand workers.

75
00:02:19,170 --> 00:02:21,530
A worker lives about six weeks.

76
00:02:22,030 --> 00:02:24,450
They keep the nursery near ninety three degrees

77
00:02:24,050 --> 00:02:25,350
on hot days,

78
00:02:25,370 --> 00:02:27,790
cooling it with water they spread and fan

79
00:02:27,390 --> 00:02:28,150
dry.

80
00:02:28,690 --> 00:02:29,670
Six weeks.

81
00:02:29,810 --> 00:02:31,950
That is all the time one bee gets.

82
00:02:32,070 --> 00:02:34,830
And the colony outlives every one of them.

83
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:36,430
The verdict.

84
00:02:36,870 --> 00:02:39,030
On the charge of keeping the world fed,

85
00:02:38,910 --> 00:02:40,830
the honeybee is found essential.

86
00:02:41,270 --> 00:02:43,410
Not because one bee is strong.

87
00:02:43,530 --> 00:02:45,610
Because none of them works alone.

88
00:02:46,170 --> 00:02:47,370
Case closed.

89
00:02:47,530 --> 00:02:49,530
Produced by Relentless Aaron.

